WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.226
Four islands, one volcanic chain: the Pitcairn Islands of the South Pacific.

00:00:06.326 --> 00:00:09.786
They lie roughly halfway between New Zealand and South America,

00:00:09.786 --> 00:00:12.038
among the most isolated islands on Earth.

00:00:13.138 --> 00:00:18.262
The four islands are the tops of volcanoes that rose from the seafloor over the past

00:00:18.262 --> 00:00:19.603
sixteen million years.

00:00:19.603 --> 00:00:24.910
Oeno is about sixteen million years old, Henderson thirteen, Ducie eight, and Pitcairn,

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.861
the youngest, under one million.

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:30.417
Henderson's story is the most instructive.

00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.341
When Pitcairn grew, less than a million years ago,

00:00:33.341 --> 00:00:36.148
its weight bent the ocean plate down beneath it,

00:00:36.148 --> 00:00:39.423
and pushed up a bulge about two hundred kilometres away.

00:00:39.423 --> 00:00:42.522
Henderson, then an ordinary atoll, sat on that bulge,

00:00:42.522 --> 00:00:45.446
and was lifted about thirty metres out of the sea.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:51.407
From space you can see the result: a flat limestone plateau, the old lagoon floor,

00:00:51.407 --> 00:00:53.837
ringed by cliffs that were once the reef.

00:00:54.937 --> 00:00:57.186
Pitcairn itself is a steep volcanic cone,

00:00:57.186 --> 00:01:00.203
only four and a half square kilometres above sea level,

00:01:00.203 --> 00:01:03.494
but rising from a base more than three thousand metres deep.
